diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage. From the Office VLAN (192.168.3.0/24), office_pc (192.168.3.2) cleanly reaches the Internet Lab hosts: ping to 128.168.0.2 and 128.168.0.3 returns 0% loss with ~1–2 ms RTT. From the Principle VLAN (192.168.4.0/24), pc0 (192.168.4.2) also successfully pings 128.168.0.2/0.3 with 0% loss. Additional checks from another Office host (enquiry) confirm the same.

The WAN path is in place. Router1 (the default gateway for 192.168.3.0/24 and 192.168.4.0/24) has:
- eth3 up at 20.20.0.1/8 toward router2, and a RIP-learned route for 128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2 on eth3 (proto rip, metric 20).
- eth2 up at 10.10.0.2/8 toward router0.
Interface counters show traffic on both uplinks, and the routing table includes the expected entries:
“128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2 dev eth3 proto rip” along with local subnets.

Mechanism: Traffic from Office/Principle enters router1 (eth0/eth1), is forwarded over the r1–r2 link (eth3, 20.20.0.0/8) to router2, then onto lan_internet where 128.168.0.x resides. The successful end-to-end ICMP confirms forwarding across the inter-router WAN.

Root cause: No active fault detected; the earlier symptom appears transient or user-side.

Corrective fix: No action required. If the issue recurs, monitor the r1–r2 link (eth3 on router1) and RIP adjacencies for flaps, but at present the WAN/uplink toward 128.168.0.0/16 is up and healthy. === END ===